ZTE F50 / MU300 on OpenWrt
==========================

Device: ZTE F50 (also sold as MU300), a 5G pocket router. SoC Unisoc UMS9620
("qogirn6pro", Cortex-A76 x4 + A55 x4, arm64), board ums9620_2h10_feimao, eMMC,
USB-C for power and USB networking, a Marlin3 (SC2355) Wi-Fi/BT chip on PCIe, no
Ethernet, no battery (the U30 Air is the same board with a battery).

Everything below is measured on hardware by the mu300-linux project
(https://github.com/dikeckaan/mu300-linux, MIT / GPL-2.0). This target exists
because OpenWrt's own kernel cannot boot the board: mainline Linux has no
UMS9620 platform support, and the drivers that make the board work live in that
project as a port of Unisoc's 5.4 vendor kernel.


1. How the device boots, and why this target looks unusual
----------------------------------------------------------

There is no U-Boot, no partition table to write and no OpenWrt sysupgrade path:

    LK (ZTE's bootloader, slot b, tries=2)
      -> Android boot image from boot_b: wrapped Image + initramfs
           -> /init (the initramfs of mu300-linux, boot/init)
                -> loads the rest, finds the root filesystem,
                   restores Android's slot in misc, switch_root

* The kernel and the initramfs are packed the way Android packs them, into an
  Android boot image (header v4, LZ4-legacy ramdisk), and written to a boot
  slot. This target therefore produces a wrapped `Image`, not a flashable
  firmware image.
* The device tree is NOT part of the kernel. LK merges the vendor dtbo and
  passes the result to the kernel from the stock `vendor_boot` partition. A
  minimal device tree of our own was tried and hung in setup_machine_fdt, so
  this target has no DTS and must not grow one.
* LK ignores the kernel command line a Linux kernel would normally read, so the
  kernel is built with CONFIG_CMDLINE_FORCE and its own command line in the
  config (see config-6.18): earlycon on 0x20210000, console=ttyS1,
  rdinit=/init, panic=5.
* LK always copies the kernel to 0x80080000, which is not 2 MiB aligned. A
  kernel since 5.8 cannot start there, so image/wrap-image.py prepends a stub
  that branches to 0x80200000. The kernel artifact of this target is that
  wrapped image (`*-Image.lk`).
* The root filesystem is not a partition either: it lives in the free area of
  the eMMC after `userdata` (on the tested 64 GB device: offset 27762098176,
  ext4, label `mu300root`; an SD card labelled `mu300sd` is used instead when
  one is present). boot/init finds it with a loop device and switch_roots into
  it. Because of this, `sysupgrade` cannot work and the image is installed onto
  that filesystem, not flashed.


2. What this target builds
--------------------------

  * `openwrt-ums9620-generic-zte_f50-ext4-Image.lk`
      the 6.18 kernel, wrapped for LK (see above). The `ext4` in the name is
      OpenWrt tagging the device image with the root filesystem type of the
      image set; it says nothing about the kernel.
  * `openwrt-ums9620-generic-zte_f50-targz-rootfs.tar.gz`
      the OpenWrt userland, with the kernel modules its kmod-* packages
      install in /lib/modules/6.18.60/ (flat, as procd's kmodloader and the
      MU300 initramfs both expect them).
  * `openwrt-ums9620-generic-rootfs.tar.gz`
      the same root filesystem without the device tag.

CONFIG_TARGET_ROOTFS_EXT4FS has to stay on in the build configuration: the
device image rule needs the ext4 root filesystem to exist, and with it a
`rootfs.ext4` can also be written straight to the eMMC area.

The Android boot image is assembled outside this tree, by the tool that has
been tested on the device, from these artifacts plus the files that only exist
on your own unit:

    MU300_LINUX=/path/to/mu300-linux
    python3 $MU300_LINUX/boot/build-boot-image.py \
        --stock-boot dumps/boot_a.img --misc-head dumps/misc-head.bin \
        --kernel  bin/targets/ums9620/generic/openwrt-ums9620-generic-zte_f50-ext4-Image.lk \
        --modules <dir with the .ko built for this kernel> \
        --busybox busybox --logdw logdw \
        --ueventd-perms android-vendor/ueventd-perms.sh \
        --android-subset android-subset \
        --out boot-linux-slotb.img

(`dumps/`, `android-subset/` and `busybox` are made by that project's
tools/backup-device.sh and android-vendor/extract-subset.sh; the rootfs goes
onto the eMMC area with its installer or with `mu300-update`.) Nothing here is
written to boot_a, and LK falls back to Android if Linux does not come up.


3. Kernel
---------

Version 6.18.60 — the same release mu300-linux's mainline port is built and
tested against, which is why the port applies here unchanged.

patches-6.18/
  0000-sprd-ums9620-port.patch
      The port itself, generated from mu300-linux upstream/port/: the drivers
      of Unisoc's 5.4 vendor kernel adapted to the 6.18 APIs and hooked into
      the tree — UMS9620 clock controller, cpufreq, devfreq/top DVFS, thermal
      (tsensor, CPU hotplug cooling), UMP9620/UMP9621 regulators and MFD,
      PMIC syscon/7sreset/soc_id, pcie-sprd (Marlin3 Wi-Fi/BT on PCIe),
      sdhci-sprd (ordering of the MMIO accessors, the eMMC as mmc0, the SD
      slot), USB PHY/BC1.2/Type-C, LEDs, the PMIC watchdog, the SoC TRNG, and
      the device-tree compatibles of the vendor tree.
  0001..0032
      mu300-linux's own kernel patches, unchanged and in its order: PMIC reset
      through spi-sprd-adi (PSCI SYSTEM_RESET never returns on this firmware),
      eMMC/SD ordering and HS400ES fallback, USB gadget NCM fixes, the battery
      and charger drivers of the U30 Air, and so on. They apply on top of the
      port, exactly as they do there.

      The numbers are theirs, which is why there is no 0022: that one was
      the reserved-memory patch of the call-audio work, removed with it
      (mu300-linux 238a4157). Two of the 32 come from their 6.18-only
      directory (0007, 0028); the rest apply to every kernel they build.

config-6.18 is that project's tested configuration (upstream/mu300-mainline.config)
with a note of what must not change. Its symbols that the generic config also
names win over the generic config, as usual for a target config.


4. What is NOT here, and why
----------------------------

The board has no in-tree drivers for three things that matter, and they are
out-of-tree modules of mu300-linux rather than something this target can build:

  * Wi-Fi and Bluetooth (sprd_wlan_combo, sprdbt_tty, wcn_bsp): Unisoc/realme
    vendor sources, built there with their patches. Without them the hotspot
    cannot come up, and wlan0 in /etc/config/network stays absent.
  * the modem stack (sipa, SIPC, the AT channel) and the Android vendor chroot
    that boots the modem, plus the MU300 toolkit in /opt/mu300 that drives it
    (mobile-data, mu300-sim, ...). Without them there is no wan and no mobile
    data, which is why /etc/config/network has no wan interface.
  * the Mali GPU (kbase) and its userspace.

A working OpenWrt image for this device therefore is a combination of what this
target builds (kernel + userland) with those modules and files, as mu300-linux
assembles it. Two ways to combine them, both tested there:

  * install this target's rootfs.tar.gz as the system and copy the modules into
    /lib/modules/<release>/, or
  * keep building the image with that project's openwrt/build-rootfs.sh and
    only take this target's kernel (that is where the kernel work happens).


5. Building
-----------

    make menuconfig     # Target: Unisoc UMS9620 (T760) / Generic / ZTE F50
    make -j$(nproc)

The kernel is large but unremarkable to build; nothing in this target needs a
vendor toolchain (LLVM's ThinLTO, which mu300-linux uses for the 5.4 kernel, is
not needed for 6.18).

Two things about the kernel configuration are worth knowing before touching it:

  * It is written out in full (see the top of config-6.18). A fragment would
    make kbuild's syncconfig ask questions, and a build has no terminal.
  * CONFIG_KERNEL_WERROR has to be off (generic's default). The ported drivers
    carry warnings the vendor kernel tolerated, a non-static function without a
    prototype in pcie-sprd-misc.c being the first one gcc reports, and
    mu300-linux builds this kernel with clang.

Not verified from this tree: no one has booted an image built here on hardware
yet. The artifacts are byte-compatible with what the tested build produces (same
kernel version, same port, same wrap, same rootfs layout) but a first boot must
be done as a trial boot (mu300-linux boot/flash-trial.sh) with Android's slot
untouched.


6. Provenance
-------------

The ported drivers and the patches carry their origin in their headers; the
port, the patches and the boot format come from mu300-linux, which is MIT and
GPL-2.0 licensed. Do not add a device tree, a sysupgrade recipe or a whole-disk
image for this board: all three have been tried on the device and fail.
